  "query_preview": "..\nturKEY MESSAGE 1...An active weather pattern is likely from late  \ntonight into the weekend. Forecast confidence on any particular \nweather system is low, but travelers should continue to monitor \nthis period for potential travel disruptions.\n\nturKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nCoastal low has developed off of the SC/NC coast and is fcst to\nhead inland today in area of greatest pressure falls. This low\npressure will track just inland of the eastern seaboard and\nspread precipitation into swrn areas around midnight tonight. \n\nBy the time precipitation arrives surface temperatures will\nlikely to have risen above freezing south of Rte 9, aka the\nAirline Rd. Areas from about Bangor east toward Princeton should\nbegin as rain and remain that way throughout the event. Having\nsaid that however, cannot rule out slick spots on bridges late\ntonight over interior Downeast. \n\nAs precip spreads north thru daybreak sfc temps will be warming\ninto the upr 20s leading to frzg rain in the morning with total\nice amounts ranging from a glaze to as much as 0.10 inches by\nthe time temps are able to warm above freezing. The time of this\nlooks to range around 7am Friday in the Dover area to as late as\nnoon in the North Woods. \n\nWrap around snow showers are expected over the northwest Friday\nnight into Saturday morning with rain showers expected over the\nremainder of nrn areas\n\nturKEY MESSAGE 2...Dangerous marine conditions and possible \ncoastal impacts around the time of the midday high tide on \nFriday.\n\nturKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThe high tide 17z on Friday will be the 3rd highest tide for the\nmonth of November. The maximum surge values will be near the low\ntide on Friday morning with a surge of 0.5 to 0.8 feet at\nFriday/s midday high tide. With seas of 6-10 feet and wave\nperiod of > 10s cannot rule out splashover along the roads\nclosest to the shore.",
